",,True,-1,"Alright, so I have this theorem to simplify: s \ t ∪ t \ s equals (s ∪ t) \ (s ∩ t). Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Maybe I can use set operations and properties to break it down step by step.

First, I know that s \ t is the same as s intersect t's complement, right? So s \ t = s ∩ t⁰. Similarly, t \ s is t ∩ s⁰. So the union of s \ t and t \ s would be (s ∩ t⁰) ∪ (t ∩ s⁰). 

Now, I recall that this is one of the distributive properties. The union of two intersections can be simplified using the distributive law of union over intersection. Let me see: (s ∩ t⁰) ∪ (t ∩ s⁰) equals (s ∪ t) ∩ (t⁰ ∪ s⁰). Wait, is that right? Let me double-check that step. Yeah, I think that's correct.

So, (s ∩ t⁰) ∪ (t ∩ s⁰) simplifies to (s ∪ t) ∩ (t⁰ ∪ s⁰). Now, t⁰ ∪ s⁰ is the same as (s ∩ t)⁰, by De Morgan's law. So, this becomes (s ∪ t) ∩ (s ∩ t)⁰.

Putting it all together: (s ∪ t) ∩ (s ∩ t)⁰ is exactly what the original theorem states. So, s \ t ∪ t \ s equals (s ∪ t) \ (s ∩ t). That seems to work out.

Let me just go over it one more time to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Start by converting the set differences to intersections with complements, then apply the distributive law, and finally use De Morgan's law to simplify the expression altogether. Yeah, that all checks out. I think this is a solid proof.

Ah, and since both sets are subsets of each other, that should be enough to show they are equal. First, that s \ t ∪ t \ s is a subset of (s ∪ t) \ (s ∩ t), and then the other way around. Maybe I can use that approach to make the proof clearer.

