",,True,-1,"List of subgroups generated by elements of a normal subgroup.

We consider a normal subgroup \( H \) of a group \( G \) and an element \( x \) in \( G \). We are interested in the subgroups of \( G \) generated by elements of the form \( xhx^{-1} \) where \( h \in H \).

First, we observe that the union of all such subgroups forms a subgroup of \( G \). This is because the union is closed under the group operation and taking inverses. Each individual subgroup is generated by a single element, and their union is contained within any subgroup that contains all elements of the form \( xhx^{-1} \).

Next, we show that this union is actually the set of all elements of the form \( xhx^{-1} \) where \( h \in H \). We use the fact that each element in this union is of the form \( xhx^{-1} \), and that these elements satisfy the subgroup properties.

Using these observations, we deduce that the subgroup generated by these elements has an index in \( G \) that is bounded by the index of \( H \) itself in \( G \). This is due to the coset decomposition and the fact that each coset can be associated with an element of this subgroup.

Finally, we apply these results to conclude that \( G \) having a normal subgroup \( H \) of finite index implies that \( G \) is finite if and only if the subgroup generated by conjugates of \( H \) is finite. This establishes a relationship between the finiteness of \( G \) and the structure of its normal subgroups.
