",,True,-1,"Take a homomorphism φ from G to H, and let S and T be subgroups of H with S ≤ T. We want to show that the pullback of S under φ is less than or equal to the pullback of T under φ.

First, recall that the pullback of a subgroup S under φ, denoted comap φ S, is defined as {x ∈ G | φ x ∈ S}. Similarly, comap φ T is {x ∈ G | φ x ∈ T}.

Given that S is a subgroup of T, every element of S is also an element of T. Therefore, if an element x of G is in comap φ S, meaning φ x is in S, then φ x is automatically in T, so x is also in comap φ T.

This shows that every element of comap φ S is in comap φ T, which means comap φ S ≤ comap φ T.
