",,True,-1,"Product of Normal Subgroups

Now, we know that the product of two normal subgroupso K and H of G is also a normal subgroup. The quotient group G/(KHis isomorphic to (G/K) × (G/H) if and only if K and H are normal and K ∩ H = {e}.

We consider the map φ: G → (G/K) × (G/H) defined by φ(g) = (gK, gH). We need to show that this map is an surjective homomorphism with kernel K ∩ H. Then, by the First Isomorphism Theorem, G/(K ∩ H) is isomorphic to (G/K) × (G/H).
